#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "2bcc7bb9-1b5e-5858-811d-1e5c14f0bc29" :question/math-academy-id "e-8288" :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_6[t])=6+1=7.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_n[t])=n+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152030 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "e1ae146f-04ae-5662-9ea3-178a23b3c366" :question/math-academy-id "e-8289" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9?$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n\n- $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "6d2ba662-f58e-56ec-bf87-c1f26c12c1c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8290" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$\n\n- $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $50 \\times 100$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$ and $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^{100}) = 100\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})) = 50\\cdot 100 = 5\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\},\\quad n\\in \\mathbb N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n a_i t^i = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a_i = 0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots, n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in \\mathbb N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "61fa85c6-5b27-5f81-a7ba-363eb68e8d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8315" :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 6$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$ contains $3\\cdot 2= 6$ elements, and is given by\n\n![](images/f2/f2ab6a78388c9be5a4b46d63916c8e299cb95746547189ed9fe2783938c007dc.png)\n\nHowever, we're asked to find the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ not $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIf we were to repeat the same process above for the vector space $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ we would have $30$ matrices in our basis, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{5\\times 6}(\\mathbb{R})) = 5\\cdot 6 =30.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})) = n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e3157e-c1fe-5d1b-ad52-732d9e91a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241349} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3b14ba-f7ee-53b8-bb9e-930a6892e7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242020} {:answer/id #uuid "a295a99f-a442-5b1c-850a-902695aec1a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242602} {:answer/id #uuid "c10a882c-6981-5092-9e0e-0e344cd07b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242871} {:answer/id #uuid "f76477fb-7405-5db0-bca5-b7944f200234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13e3157e-c1fe-5d1b-ad52-732d9e91a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241349} :answer-field/id #uuid "223ceb91-14ae-5a21-a208-e1e86c099cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d133675-64d5-50d6-9a87-de7265046742" :question/math-academy-id "q-106868" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${R}_{24}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $24?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{24}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{24}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{24}[t])=24+1=25.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b9d3a8-da80-5055-a4ac-61a8de4abec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397879} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5db82b-df8d-5504-91e7-fcc98356c581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397880} {:answer/id #uuid "9af72902-6c61-5539-95d7-3ad5251165aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397881} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb6755f-48c1-5f6b-9cd2-eb9cbe4c57c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397882} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba279e7-9cb7-5403-9454-8ad5b15d2a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22b9d3a8-da80-5055-a4ac-61a8de4abec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397879} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ee78a5-d74a-542f-9cb8-9d156de061c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90418efb-5f81-5124-80f6-ce7467b7ec0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106888" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{8}(R),$ the vector space of all $8\\times 8$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{8}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{8}(R))={8}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f50e01-4432-5abc-a190-7e72cdc3873a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461479} {:answer/id #uuid "26b77efa-1ca5-50e3-a239-917ffd78fa11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f50e01-4432-5abc-a190-7e72cdc3873a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461479} :answer-field/id #uuid "d618925b-ccec-5382-aafc-ee623f24eae3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3755b38e-d3c3-5fd6-aa30-ce41d9c8f023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461481} {:answer/id #uuid "0113c910-9d82-5496-a7a5-6245045c74c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0113c910-9d82-5496-a7a5-6245045c74c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461482} :answer-field/id #uuid "6af77266-4c24-5185-b7d2-41604c2df381" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e21d3928-a529-5423-a7ee-52349098e089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461483} {:answer/id #uuid "a21066ee-90f4-5cd6-a2c8-b896111b61f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a21066ee-90f4-5cd6-a2c8-b896111b61f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461484} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e400bec-f914-5615-b660-6b8705f12fef" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19d4d0ee-ad5b-53e7-956e-b02eb6cf177b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106901" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $20?$\n- **I.** ${R}_{20}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $20$\n- **II.** ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- **III.** ${R}^{20}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{20}[t])=20+1=21\\ne 20.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R))=4\\cdot 5=20.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{20})=20.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54cbfca0-bff0-52dc-a7d3-f7f9ea5acb20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468858} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8db7bb-912a-549b-8dc7-d4e68d879053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab8db7bb-912a-549b-8dc7-d4e68d879053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468859} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc9b4d17-b28a-526f-8892-83a499708178" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470041} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c5112c1-4747-5e8b-a12b-430126d5ce9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468860} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8614be-f12e-5507-b395-aa4a960dd2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c5112c1-4747-5e8b-a12b-430126d5ce9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468860} :answer-field/id #uuid "e370841a-5acd-57e5-9802-a92d25d0133e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470042} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1bff07-aba3-5a99-aece-07d510737e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468862} {:answer/id #uuid "1a402636-9d56-5283-8679-8961a991afe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae1bff07-aba3-5a99-aece-07d510737e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468862} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea2f7538-5026-507b-a35a-db7e12ed1e93" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a2340ea-4d69-565f-a0a6-1a2327573430" :question/math-academy-id "q-106904" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $11?$\n- **I.** ${R}_{10}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $10$\n- **II.** ${\\text{M}}_{2\\times 6}(R),$ the vector space of all $2\\times 6$ matrices over $R$\n- **III.** ${R}^{10}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{10}[t])=10+1=11.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{2\\times 6}(R))=2\\cdot 6=12\\ne 11.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{10})=10\\ne 11.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101d4aef-a0cb-5f59-804c-2d0912201356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461485} {:answer/id #uuid "79a8eb56-c474-574e-bfc9-b522256a029e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "101d4aef-a0cb-5f59-804c-2d0912201356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461485} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce30771f-f100-546b-a92b-fb508272ed27" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed8774e9-ebe2-5c6e-85cd-30049680d599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461487} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8ccde8-41e3-5196-ae3c-09832fdc7f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc8ccde8-41e3-5196-ae3c-09832fdc7f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461488} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6f63d3c-95cd-558d-bbab-210730504255" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462497} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c06bef4-3a80-5865-b652-4990c086ea64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461489} {:answer/id #uuid "068ca4a0-476a-53a3-9006-a1ac154420c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "068ca4a0-476a-53a3-9006-a1ac154420c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461490} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b82d7b2-4328-5c48-a37f-e8fdf88d664e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28ab3efd-4668-556c-a241-6c3f64cf9ac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-106916" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n- **I.** ${ℓ}^{1},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the absolute values of the terms are finite\n- **II.** ${R}_{3000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3\\,000$\n- **III.** ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R),$ the vector space of all $50\\times 60$ matrices over $R$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in {1,2,…,n},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{1}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{3000}[t]$ and  ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{3000}[t])=3\\,000+1=3\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R))=50\\cdot 60=3\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c610096-f546-51ed-933a-3503175a90be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399284} {:answer/id #uuid "0b54db3c-8d6d-526f-a175-03ba6ce2af5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399285} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0e9dfa-1e7d-5276-a512-39501b2a2767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399286} {:answer/id #uuid "88dcd79e-fa94-516e-9b4f-1e0158b64a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399287} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb12f7f-6eed-5940-a40a-4ec06ffa1f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb12f7f-6eed-5940-a40a-4ec06ffa1f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399288} :answer-field/id #uuid "5009b981-acee-537f-a90c-d9fc4554b09c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20513819-0985-5cdd-a8ca-960c927c6ced" :question/math-academy-id "q-95216" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 4$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R))=6\\cdot 4=24.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19295612-c37d-5d12-a2bd-d9e3d3190165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357973} {:answer/id #uuid "43d957d6-a406-5fc0-89ac-e558f5385934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358332} {:answer/id #uuid "8291e989-30da-596c-9329-97f50fc939b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358897} {:answer/id #uuid "840d738a-d64e-59df-aa33-1da6f0865be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358913} {:answer/id #uuid "906172b7-c7a4-5308-bdb0-d00e129149b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19295612-c37d-5d12-a2bd-d9e3d3190165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357973} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0266e5-db5e-546e-b8af-973195c37e19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4991b06-e003-5ecf-b7e7-fe43aa760937" :question/math-academy-id "q-95217" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 2}(R),$ the vector space of all $10\\times 2$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 2}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{10\\times 2}(R))=10\\cdot 2=20.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021e2b2f-1cfa-5e7f-a34a-b7f2570312cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469758} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb72ca2-146b-5bb8-8385-38863e97843d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdb72ca2-146b-5bb8-8385-38863e97843d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469759} :answer-field/id #uuid "29eb04aa-7838-546a-bb99-32c656373b29" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470491} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4771ff23-e6f3-5c09-9038-6c2677900baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469760} {:answer/id #uuid "e7201a40-a402-5dd1-be24-316d02c7b70f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7201a40-a402-5dd1-be24-316d02c7b70f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469761} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e735b4-4170-55a6-b050-658c79ff7a90" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d82bb2f-ac86-5000-956c-61719812870d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469762} {:answer/id #uuid "e939c23c-8529-587d-843f-30d45e082086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e939c23c-8529-587d-843f-30d45e082086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469763} :answer-field/id #uuid "0531dddc-ea4a-5f2c-bd79-4fdd1a0039fc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e94aa3d-a675-513e-b4ce-d62e1028540d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95240" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n- **I.** ${R}_{1000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $1\\,000$\n- **II.** ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 500$ matrices over $R$\n- **III.** $C,$ as a vector space over $R$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${R}_{1000}[t]$ and ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{1000}[t])=1\\,000+1=1\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R))=200\\cdot 500=100\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $C$ is also finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of complex numbers is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,i}\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1+b\\cdot i=0\n$$\n\nif and only if $a=0$ and $b=0.$ Since our vector space is also spanned by this linearly independent subset, we have that $\\text{dim}(C)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
